# STANDING REMINDERS FOR AWS (read every time we touch the cloud)

**WHAT THIS FILE DOES (plain English):**
This is the rulebook for talking to Amazon's computers (AWS). Bridger's
app server lives there. These rules keep us from guessing, from putting
passwords in chat, and from building cloud stuff by hand that we cannot
undo. Same idea as the Cursor standing reminders in `guide-rules.mdc`.
Re-read this before any AWS task. Do not skip it.

These rules apply to every AWS task, every session. When a rule here
conflicts with a one-off prompt, ask before proceeding. When this file
and `INFRASTRUCTURE.md` disagree, `INFRASTRUCTURE.md` wins on stack
choices. Secrets stay out of chat, git, and client code. Always.

**COPY RULE: NO EM DASHES IN AWS NAMES.** Never use an em dash (the long
dash character) in AWS resource names or descriptions. Use a hyphen
instead. Same copy rule as the rest of Bridger.

1 · Talk through the safe door first
Use the AWS helper in Cursor (the AWS MCP Server) when it is available.
That path is watched and logged, so we can see what happened later
(sandboxed execution, observability, and audit logging). If that helper
is down, use the AWS command line (AWS CLI) directly. Do not invent a
third way in.

2 · Read the how-to before you start
Before you begin an AWS task, check whether a matching AWS skill exists
(the short playbook for that job). Load it with `retrieve_skill` and
follow it instead of guessing from memory. A skill beats general
knowledge every time.

3 · Do not guess
If you are unsure about an AWS detail (what a setting is called, who
is allowed to do what, size limits, or error codes), look it up in the
docs. Say so out loud if you cannot confirm. A wrong cloud change is
expensive. A "I do not know yet" is cheap.

4 · Write the cloud as code, not as one-off clicks
When you create infrastructure (servers, secrets, networks), write it
down as infrastructure-as-code (AWS CDK or CloudFormation). Do not click
around in the console or fire one-off CLI commands that nobody can
replay. Follow the Well-Architected ideas (secure, reliable, cheap
enough, easy to operate). The stack we already chose lives in
`INFRASTRUCTURE.md`. Do not re-litigate it.

5 · Passwords stay in the vault
**SECURITY.** Keys, tokens, passwords, and API secrets live in the
password vault (AWS Secrets Manager). They must never land in chat,
in git, or in the phone/web app.

- Load the `aws-secrets-manager` skill first for any secret, credential,
  API key, token, or password task. No exceptions.
- Do not call `secretsmanager get-secret-value` or
  `batch-get-secret-value`. Do not talk to the Secrets Manager Agent
  daemon yourself. Those paths pull the secret into this chat.
- Use this placeholder instead, so the secret is filled in at run time
  and never appears here:
  `{{resolve:secretsmanager:secret-id:SecretString:json-key}}`
  with `asm-exec`.

If you catch yourself about to paste a key, stop. That is a bug.
